1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,250
None of these ready-made people are me. Up
one, where I answer for myself.

2
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:00,500
Right. Let us see what I look like written
down.

3
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,750
Fairport Harbor. I have run gear out of a
dozen towns with that kind of name.

4
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:01,000
Yes. The shape of the world is not mine to
argue with.

5
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,250
Before I answer anything, let me see what
the answers cost.

6
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:01,500
A version of me with no limits is not a
person, it is a wish. Down one.

7
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:01,750
Strong in one place is paid for in
another. I would rather see the bill.

8
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:02,000
Right. Now where does this begin.

9
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,250
There is a long list here and I already
know which one is mine.

10
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:02,500
M.

11
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:02,750
I.

12
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:03,000
S. Missed. That is the honest word for
what happened to me.

13
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:03,250
Show me what is left when I ask for that.

14
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:03,500
I was on the water when it started. I came
back to a harbour with nobody left.

15
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:03,750
That is settled then. Now the trade.

16
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,000
I know the answer to this one without
reading the list.

17
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,250
F.

18
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:04,500
I.

19
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:04,750
S.

20
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:05,000
H. Fisher. Thirty-one seasons of it.

21
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,250
Show me.

22
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:05,500
A rod, a knife, waders, and my father's
watch. That last I would fight for.

23
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:05,750
That is me on paper. What else do they
want.

24
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:06,000
Two habits I will not pretend about, and
both of them are on this page.

25
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,250
N.

26
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:06,500
I.

27
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:06,750
C. Nicotine. Since I was nineteen.

28
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:07,000
Show me.

29
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,250
Smoking since nineteen. It will cost me a
bad head, and then worse.

30
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:07,500
And the other one.

31
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:07,750
C.

32
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:08,000
A.

33
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,250
F.

34
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:08,500
Show me both of them.

35
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:08,750
No. I drink it black and plenty of it, but
it never made me strange.

36
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:09,000
Black and too much of it for thirty years.
Two days of headache, then fine.

37
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:09,250
There is one on there that is not true of
me.

38
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:09,500
Clear that out first.

39
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:09,750
H. Over the top of it, then.

40
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:10,000
I.

41
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:10,250
G. High school.

42
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:10,500
Show me.

43
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:10,750
I left school at sixteen for the boats. I
will not write myself a diploma.

44
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:11,000
There is one licence on that list I have
actually held in my hand.

45
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:11,250
B.

46
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:11,500
O.

47
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:11,750
A. Boating.

48
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:12,000
Show me.

49
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:12,250
Nine years with my own boat. Forty-two
feet, and a diesel I rebuilt twice.

50
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:12,500
Now the part where I put a number on
myself.

51
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:12,750
Thirty-one years hauling traps over a
gunwale. Nobody would argue with it.

52
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:13,000
I can still put a full trap on the rail.
Ten, and that is all I claim.

53
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,250
My hands are ordinary. Never quick, only
steady, and steady is not listed.

54
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:13,500
Here is where I pay for the boats.

55
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:13,750
School ended at sixteen. Anything out of a
book comes to me slowly.

56
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:14,000
And here is what I got instead of
schooling.

57
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:14,250
I can read a change in the water at half a
mile. That I will claim.

58
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:14,500
I notice things before I have decided to.
Ten, and I will not gild it past that.

59
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:14,750
Now the part I put off. I would rather
know my weight than flatter it.

60
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:15,000
Start with the thing that will put me on
the floor.

61
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,250
B.

62
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:15,500
A.

63
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:15,750
C.

64
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:16,000
K. My back.

65
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:16,250
There it is, in a list, like a diagnosis.

66
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:16,500
Six years of it. There is a way of lifting
that puts me on the deck.

67
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:16,750
And the other thing I have always known
about myself.

68
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:17,000
S.

69
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:17,250
L.

70
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:17,500
E.

71
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:17,750
E again.

72
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:18,000
P. How I sleep.

73
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:18,250
Show me.

74
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:18,500
Thirty-one years in a bunk over a diesel.
No noise has ever woken me.

75
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:18,750
One more honest thing, and then the other
column.

76
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:19,000
A.

77
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:19,250
D.

78
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:19,500
D again.

79
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:19,750
I.

80
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:20,000
C.

81
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:20,250
T. It runs straight down my mother's side
of the family.

82
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:20,500
Show me.

83
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:20,750
That is the wrong way round. I am not the
resistant kind and never have been.

84
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:21,000
Coffee and cigarettes ran the boats. It
cost me something I loved already.

85
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:21,250
Now what is right with me. There are
fewer, and I will not pad the list.

86
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:21,500
T.

87
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:21,750
O.

88
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:22,000
U.

89
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:22,250
G.

90
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:22,500
H. I do not break easily.

91
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:22,750
Show me.

92
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:23,000
Thrown against a gunwale in February and
back at work by the afternoon.

93
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:23,250
Next.

94
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:23,500
S.

95
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:23,750
T.

96
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:24,000
O.

97
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,250
M. My stomach.

98
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:24,500
Show me.

99
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:24,750
No. The opposite of that, in fact.

100
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:25,000
There. That one.

101
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:25,250
Bait-box sandwiches and three-day chowder.
None of it ever came back up.

102
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:25,500
One more, and it is the one people always
get wrong about me.

103
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:25,750
P.

104
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:26,000
A.

105
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,250
C.

106
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:26,500
K. What I can carry.

107
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:26,750
Show me.

108
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:27,000
Everything aboard has one place it goes.
My back pays; I know how to stow.

109
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,250
That is the whole of what I am. Now what I
can do with it.

110
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:27,500
Bashing things with a stick is not a
trade. Past it.

111
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:27,750
Knives are for fish and rope. Here is the
one that has actually kept me alive.

112
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:28,000
What kills people out there is being in a
rush. I let things go past me.

113
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:28,250
Dodging reads two now. Past the
hand-to-hand.

114
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:28,500
Nor that.

115
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:28,750
I have never hit anybody in my life and I
am not going to start claiming I know how.

116
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:29,000
The bar is on unarmed now. No.

117
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,250
Archery. My father would have laughed.

118
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:29,500
There was a flare pistol in the locker.
That is all I know of firearms.

119
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:29,750
Launchers. I would not know which end to
hold.

120
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:30,000
Marksmanship. No.

121
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:30,250
Rifles. No.

122
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:30,500
A man two slips down kept one aboard. I
thought it foolish to have.

123
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:30,750
Whatever a submachine gun is, it is not
mine.

124
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:31,000
I can throw a line. That is not the same
thing and I will not pretend it is.

125
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:31,250
Applied science. That is for people who
stayed in school.

126
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:31,500
I can rewire a bilge pump and that is the
end of my electrics.

127
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:31,750
Nets, line, canvas, a half-rotten hull
made sound. This one I have earned.

128
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:32,000
Fabrication reads two of mine on top of
the one I already had. Next.

129
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:32,250
Whatever came up in the net, in a pan.
Nine years of feeding a crew.

130
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:32,500
Food handling is bought. On.

131
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:32,750
Five came with the trade already. I will
not buy more of what I have.

132
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,000
Every stitch in my own oilskins is mine.
Aboard, that job was always mine.

133
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:33,250
Tailoring is bought. Next.

134
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:33,500
Twice over the side in nine years and
twice back aboard. No wind to run.

135
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:33,750
Athletics reads two and two. Two things
left that I have actually done.

136
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:34,000
A telephone is a telephone. I do not need
a level in it.

137
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:34,250
Devices, whatever that means. Past it.

138
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:34,500
Hooks through a hand, rope burn, a man
short two fingers. I patched them.

139
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:34,750
Health care is bought. One thing left, and
it is the one I am proudest of.

140
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:35,000
I rebuilt that diesel twice. A bad bearing
I can usually hear and fix.

141
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:35,250
It has me down as a man. I am a woman, and
fifty-two years of one.

142
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:35,500
And put me in my own clothes while it is
at it.

143
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:35,750
Fifty-two years, and most of them wet.

144
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:36,000
Five.

145
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:36,250
Two. Fifty-two.

146
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:36,500
Fifty-two. Thirty-one seasons out of
Gloucester, nine on my own boat.

147
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:36,750
They ought to know what to call me.

148
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:37,000
O.

149
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:37,250
D.

150
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:37,500
E.

151
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:37,750
T.

152
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:38,000
T again.

153
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:38,250
E. Odette. Everyone who ever mattered
called me Dette.

154
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:38,500
And his name after mine.

155
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:38,750
V.

156
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:39,000
A.

157
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:39,250
C.

158
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:39,500
H.

159
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:39,750
O.

160
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:40,000
N. Vachon. My grandfather brought it down
from Riviere-du-Loup in a duffel bag.

161
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:40,250
Odette Vachon. Out of Gloucester, and
before that Riviere-du-Loup.

162
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:40,500
Let me see all of it laid out before I
sign my name to it.

163
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:40,750
Strong, slow, sharp-eyed, and not one
weapon on the page. That is me.

164
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:41,000
Yes. Camille teaches in Salem and I will
not learn that from in here.

165
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:41,250
First thing in a strange room: find out if
anything is looking back.

166
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:41,500
Nothing alive in here but me. I will take
that and stop counting caps.

167
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:41,750
Thirty-one years of reading charts first.
I want to know where water is.

168
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:42,000
West Rutland, and not a drop of salt water
anywhere. I am well inland.

169
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,250
Sit tight and listen. That is most of what
fishing ever was.

170
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:52,250
My father's watch has an alarm. Waiting is
what I want from it now.

171
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:54,250
Five minutes. If anything in here heard
me, it will say so by then.

172
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:54,500
Gargling. That is a throat that no longer
works, and it is close.

173
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:54,750
Northwest, and northwest of me is a shut
door. Take a sounding first.

174
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:55,000
Caps, balls, a till nobody wants. None of
that is what I asked for.

175
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:55,250
Nothing alive on the page, and I heard it
plain. It is behind a wall.

176
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:56,250
Twenty-six racks, eight windows, and not
one tap in the whole building.

177
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:57,250
Get off this carpet. East, toward the
light, with furniture between us.

178
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:58,250
One step and I have already made noise.
Nothing to do but keep going.

179
00:00:58,250 --> 00:00:58,500
Cloth hanging on the rack below. I stop
beside them, not into them.

180
00:00:58,500 --> 00:00:58,750
Cloth on a rack, and nothing on my arms.
Skin covered is skin kept.

181
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:02,750
A bag is also whatever stands in it. Look
inside before I heave it up.

182
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:03,750
Empty. But it is space, and space I have
none of. Over the shoulder.

183
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:05,750
Four pounds heavier already. Now the
counter, where the useful things go.

184
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:06,750
Quiet as waders allow. This floor tells
the room exactly where I am.

185
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:07,750
Two seconds for that step, not one.
Something underfoot, or the bag.

186
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:08,000
The till stands open with fifteen dollars
in it. I want a bottle instead.

187
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
A car. A whole car out on the tarmac. But
the counter first, in order.

188
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Nothing but dollars I cannot drink. The
car, and by a door not a window.

189
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,000
One pace, one second. On the water you
count seconds too, for weather.

190
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:11,250
The green thing is still on the rack. I
mark it and come back if I can.

191
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:12,250
I go to cut the corner and the rack stops
me. I counted the line wrong.

192
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:13,250
Straight down the aisle then, and no more
cleverness. The rack wins that argument.

193
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:15,250
A jacket on the end of the line. Two paces
off my way if I come back.

194
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:17,250
Now the line has ended for certain. I will
take the corner I was refused.

195
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:17,500
Pale and squat by the wall. A bin, or a
basin with an inch left in it.

196
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Close enough now to reach it. I will not
guess. Guessing is how people drown.

197
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:19,500
Nothing in it. There is no water in a golf
shop and I knew that.

198
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:20,500
A patch of dark in front of the door.
Better it does not see me first.

199
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,500
Hand on the handle, not a shoulder. Quiet
out means quiet back in.

200
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,500
It swung without a sound. Stand in the gap
and look before crossing.

201
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,500
Three red cars and nobody on the tarmac.
Steady, no running, eyes up.

202
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,500
Out in the open with nothing between me
and the treeline. Quickly.

203
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:27,500
Tarmac under waders is a sound I will not
learn to like.

204
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,500
Third one. My back is already telling me
about a bag with nothing in it.

205
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:30,500
Four seats, a whole screen, the boot
toward me. People forget boots.

206
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:31,500
Two seconds a pace out here. Waders were
never made for tarmac.

207
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:31,750
Over the yellow line. Nobody is left to
mind the gap it was painted for.

208
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:32,750
Close enough to touch the paint. Let us
see what a golfer leaves behind.

209
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:33,000
A painted bay line between me and it. My
arm is not that long. One more.

210
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:33,250
Warm already where the sun has been on it.
Now find out what is inside.

211
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:33,500
A squirrel, east, and my mouth watered. It
will not hold still for me.

212
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:33,750
It offers to remember where the car is. I
know where the car is.

213
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:34,000
Faulty motor, bare boot, petrol I cannot
carry. Four dry seats, though.

214
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:34,250
A golf course has a pond in it. I have a
rod and fifteen baits.

215
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:34,500
Goods first, always. I do not want goods.
I want a shape of water.

216
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:34,750
Not goods. Not the living. The ground.
Show me the ground.

217
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:35,000
Eight hundred tiles of forest and a
hundred and eighty of underbrush.

218
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:35,250
Turn the page. If there is water here it
will be in the far half.

219
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:35,500
Forest floor six paces south. A wood in
May has things a woman can eat.

220
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:35,750
Keep going. The greens are at the far end,
and water follows greens.

221
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:36,000
Sixty-eight of green, three flags, a whole
wood, and still no water.

222
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:36,250
Nine willows. Willow keeps its feet wet.
That means standing water.

223
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:37,250
No water in fifty-nine kinds of ground.
But underbrush is seven paces.

224
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:38,250
South. Off the tarmac, over the kerb, into
the green.

225
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:39,250
South. Off the tarmac, over the kerb, into
the green.

226
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:40,250
South. Off the tarmac, over the kerb, into
the green.

227
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:41,250
The kerb, and then real ground. A leafy
thing four paces down.

228
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:43,250
Grass. My knees are grateful and my back
is not, and both are right.

229
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,250
Softer underfoot and quieter, which suits
me. Two more paces.

230
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:45,500
Grass takes two seconds a pace where
tarmac took one. Thicker than it looks.

231
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:51,500
I am standing in it. Patience is most of
the difference between meals.

232
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:51,750
Yes. Yes, of course yes.

233
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:52,000
Two handfuls of leaves. Greens hold water,
and water I have none of.

234
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,000
There they are, listed as mine to take.
Mark them.

235
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Marked. Take them.

236
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Two handfuls is not a day's eating.
Another patch six along and two down.

237
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Three seconds to get out. Getting out
costs what getting in did.

238
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
East along the seam where grass meets
tarmac. I can see both ways.

239
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,000
The road below runs east and west and
nothing is moving on it.

240
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Three. Nearly on it.

241
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:04,250
Beside it now. I will search it from here
rather than wade in again.

242
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:04,500
Nothing in that one. Two nets come up full
and the third holds a boot.

243
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:04,750
The course lies north, four squares deep,
with pale blue at the top.

244
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:05,000
The square above me is the car park
itself. Of course it is. Keep going north.

245
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:05,250
Car park, then the course. Two squares up
and I am on the fairway.

246
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:05,500
Still the course. It is a big one.

247
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:05,750
Four squares of golf course. One more and
I am on the blue.

248
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:06,000
A forest trail is a way through a wood
without fighting the wood.

249
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:06,250
Swamp. Five squares north, and nine
willows saying it from the ground.

250
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:06,500
I do not need a colour. I need five
letters I will trust at first light.

251
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:06,750
W. A woman writing on a chart with one
finger, like my father did.

252
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:07,000
W, A. He wrote his in pencil so he could
rub out a mark when a bank shifted.

253
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:07,250
W, A, T.

254
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:07,500
W, A, T, E. One more and it is written
down where it cannot fall out of my head.

255
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:07,750
WATER, on the chart and out of my head,
where I cannot lose it.

256
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:09,750
Tomorrow is settled. Nothing to carry
water in, so home and shut the door.

257
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:11,750
Thirteen east and twelve south to walk
back. Round the cars, not through.

258
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:12,750
Two seconds a pace on grass. It adds up,
and my back is keeping the tally.

259
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:14,750
Out, round a car, down to the road and
back. A morning's work already.

260
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:15,750
Over the kerb onto the hard stuff. Quick
walking and loud walking both.

261
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:17,750
Both its doors hang open like a gutted
fish. I go up the west side.

262
00:02:17,750 --> 00:02:19,750
A second a pace on tarmac against two on
grass. One small favour.

263
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:20,750
Two seconds that one, so I have crossed a
painted line. Three down, five to go.

264
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:22,750
Halfway. The wall is on my left with its
windows gone dark.

265
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:24,750
Five. The door is three more on the slant,
if I have counted right.

266
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:25,750
There it is, black and open, with the bin
inside and the racks beyond.

267
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:27,750
Two more.

268
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:28,750
One more and I am on the sidewalk level
with the doorway.

269
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:29,750
Level with it. The leaf is swung back into
the dark. In I go.

270
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:30,750
Under a roof. I did not know how much I
minded until it was back.

271
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:31,750
Now shut it. A door left open is an
invitation, and I am careful.

272
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:33,750
Black as a hold with the hatch on. But I
said I would fetch that jacket.

273
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:34,750
The bin is under my boot and slow going.
Somebody emptied it and left.

274
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:35,750
Off the bin and it charged me for the
privilege both ways. Two more.

275
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:36,000
One more and it should be off my left
shoulder.

276
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:36,250
Bright green in all this brown. Cloth
between my arms and teeth counts.

277
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:36,500
Another golf bag, and no room for it.
Promise kept, reward hollow.

278
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:36,750
This is my station: far side from the
noise, one shut door, and glass.

279
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:46,750
Wait first. The alarm is for when I
actually lie down.

280
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:48,750
Eleven hours in one sitting. A skipper out
in bad weather is not brave.

281
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:49,000
Groaning, not gargling. Lungs still
trying. Stop and find out where.

282
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:49,250
Northwest again, and both those doors were
shut. It cannot reach me.

283
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:59,250
Wait, not alarm. The alarm is for tonight.

284
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:10,250
Eleven hours and forty-four minutes of it
left. Again.

285
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:21,250
It has hit that door three times and lost.
I will not flinch all day.

286
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:32,250
Withdrawals. Since nineteen and since
twelve, and today they collect.

287
00:03:33,250 --> 00:03:33,500
The whole day went past me. Very thirsty
is a different animal.

288
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:42,500
Thirteen calories, and rotten by dawn.
Down they go, both handfuls.

289
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:42,750
It interrupts my supper to say I want a
coffee. I am aware of it.

290
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:43,000
The emptiness has gone. One handful left,
spoiled by morning anyway.

291
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Now the watch. He wore it every day and I
never once set the alarm.

292
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:52,250
It takes only its own four answers.
Ignore, then, and let me eat.

293
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:52,500
Twenty-six calories of bitter leaves. Now
the watch.

294
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:52,750
Never mind, it says. Fine. A shop floor is
all I have wanted for hours.

295
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:53,000
Yes. Boots on, rod at my hand. Goodnight,
Camille, wherever you are.

296
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Eight hours. Up when the sky goes grey,
with all the light ahead of me.

297
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:04,250
It will not even let me sleep. Stay down.
Stay down, Odette.

298
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:14,250
Yes, I am having trouble sleeping. I will
get there. Stop asking me.

299
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:25,250
It woke me to mention tobacco again. There
is none. Down.

300
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:26,500
Survive a day and find a safe place to
sleep. A golf shop floor. Well.

301
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:26,750
Four hours is not a night, and four is
when I said I would be up.

302
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:27,000
Yes. Same floor, same rack, same dark. Go
back down.

303
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Four hours puts me at first light. Then
north, to see about the willows.

304
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Again. The small hours are when it likes
to remind me. Ignore. Sleep.

305
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Twenty-five minutes of turning over. No. I
would rather sleep.

306
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:00,250
Beep-beep-beep. It worked. He would be
unbearable about it.

307
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:00,500
One day. No water found, but I know where
it is. Camille, I am still here.
